{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KEytP9LpeZMawVzDiutAio","type":"tool_result","content":"47:> — CNCF Cloud Native Definition v1.1 [source: cncf-cloud-native-definition-2026-08-23]\n161:[source: cncf-cloud-native-definition-2026-08-23]\n187:<!-- FIGURE: ch17-fig01-cloud-native-definition-characteristics -->\n382:[Omitted long matching line]\n452:- **C is correct** — verbatim from the definition's second sentence [source: cncf-cloud-native-definition-2026-08-23].\n461:**5. D.** \"This list is non-exhaustive\" appears in the definition itself, in the same sentence as the list [source: cncf-cloud-native-definition-2026-08-23].\n1641:[Omitted long matching line]\n1643:**Q2 — A.** \"public, private, hybrid cloud\" appears in the definition's first sentence [source: cncf-cloud-native-definition-2026-08-23]. **B** is the misconception the definition preemptively rules out. **C** is wrong — the definition is explicit, not silent. **D** invents a requirement the definition does not make; a platform's conformance to a Kubernetes distribution standard has nothing to do with whether it meets this definition.\n1645:**Q3 — D.** The definition's closing clause: techniques combined with robust automation \"allow engineers to make high-impact changes frequently and predictably with minimal toil and clear separation of concerns\" [source: cncf-cloud-native-definition-2026-08-23]. **A** overclaims: \"minimal toil\" is not \"no operations staff.\" **B** promises a guarantee no architecture provides. **C** invents a cost claim the definition does not make."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aa5db13d-100b-4b10-b3c7-dabe0218186d","uuid":"ec625bef-299d-4fd8-9627-534b0fdf26b1","timestamp":"2026-08-31T14:50:46.117Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"47:> — CNCF Cloud Native Definition v1.1 [source: cncf-cloud-native-definition-2026-08-23]\n161:[source: cncf-cloud-native-definition-2026-08-23]\n187:<!-- FIGURE: ch17-fig01-cloud-native-definition-characteristics -->\n382:[Omitted long matching line]\n452:- **C is correct** — verbatim from the definition's second sentence [source: cncf-cloud-native-definition-2026-08-23].\n461:**5. D.** \"This list is non-exhaustive\" appears in the definition itself, in the same sentence as the list [source: cncf-cloud-native-definition-2026-08-23].\n1641:[Omitted long matching line]\n1643:**Q2 — A.** \"public, private, hybrid cloud\" appears in the definition's first sentence [source: cncf-cloud-native-definition-2026-08-23]. **B** is the misconception the definition preemptively rules out. **C** is wrong — the definition is explicit, not silent. **D** invents a requirement the definition does not make; a platform's conformance to a Kubernetes distribution standard has nothing to do with whether it meets this definition.\n1645:**Q3 — D.** The definition's closing clause: techniques combined with robust automation \"allow engineers to make high-impact changes frequently and predictably with minimal toil and clear separation of concerns\" [source: cncf-cloud-native-definition-2026-08-23]. **A** overclaims: \"minimal toil\" is not \"no operations staff.\" **B** promises a guarantee no architecture provides. **C** invents a cost claim the definition does not make.","numLines":9,"totalLines":9}}
